WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.120
Does a receptor with an empty pocket stay silent?

2
00:00:07.980 --> 00:00:12.120
Not quite. A G-protein-coupled receptor is flexible.

3
00:00:12.820 --> 00:00:17.580
Even with nothing bound, it flickers between inactive and active shapes.

4
00:00:18.420 --> 00:00:22.320
For many receptors, a small fraction is active at any moment.

5
00:00:22.580 --> 00:00:26.300
So the receptor signals a little with no ligand at all.

6
00:00:27.040 --> 00:00:31.520
This is basal activity, and it gives a drug three ways to act.

7
00:00:32.433 --> 00:00:35.073
The first way is an agonist.

8
00:00:35.633 --> 00:00:40.753
It binds the active shape more tightly, so it holds receptors in that shape.

9
00:00:41.793 --> 00:00:44.153
Signaling rises above the basal level.

10
00:00:44.967 --> 00:00:47.467
The second is a neutral antagonist.

11
00:00:48.107 --> 00:00:50.247
It binds both shapes equally well.

12
00:00:50.707 --> 00:00:52.227
Alone, it changes nothing.

13
00:00:52.807 --> 00:00:56.287
But it occupies the pocket, so it keeps other ligands out.

14
00:00:57.100 --> 00:00:59.820
The third is an inverse agonist.

15
00:01:00.360 --> 00:01:03.100
It binds the inactive shape more tightly.

16
00:01:03.380 --> 00:01:08.540
So it pulls receptors out of the active state, and signaling falls below basal.

17
00:01:09.500 --> 00:01:14.860
In a cell with no basal activity, an antagonist and an inverse agonist look the

18
00:01:14.860 --> 00:01:19.440
same. You need a system with measurable basal activity to tell them apart.

19
00:01:20.440 --> 00:01:25.700
Many drugs long called antagonists proved to be inverse agonists when tested that way.
